('127.109.d.7.E',4,'Underground raceways are typically buried:','["At any depth", "At the depth required by code", "At the surface", "In the roof"]'::jsonb,1,'Burial depth is regulated.','original'),
('127.109.d.7.F',3,'The NEC limits how many conductors can be placed in a raceway to prevent:','["Lighter loads", "Color fading", "Overheating and damage", "Noise"]'::jsonb,2,'Fill limits.','original'),
('127.109.d.7.F',4,'The NEC rule on bends in a run of conduit between pull points limits the total to:','["180 degrees", "1080 degrees", "90 degrees", "360 degrees"]'::jsonb,3,'No more than four quarter bends.','original'),
('127.109.d.7.G',3,'Boxes on masonry surfaces are fastened using:','["Masonry anchors", "Tape", "Rope", "Staples"]'::jsonb,0,'Masonry needs anchors.','original'),
('127.109.d.7.G',4,'Conduit on a block wall is attached with:','["Glue", "Straps secured with masonry anchors", "Tape", "Nails in mortar"]'::jsonb,1,'Proper fastening.','original'),
('127.109.d.7.H',3,'When working with a conduit bender, a safety rule is to:','["Hold it overhead", "Stand on the bender", "Keep feet clear and use correct leverage", "Use no gloves"]'::jsonb,2,'Safe handling.','original'),
('127.109.d.7.H',4,'Sharp conduit ends should be:','["Painted", "Left rough", "Taped", "Reamed to remove burrs"]'::jsonb,3,'Burrs cut wire.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.A',3,'Wire sizes in the United States follow the:','["American Wire Gauge (AWG)", "Standard time", "Roman scale", "Metric tape"]'::jsonb,0,'AWG sets sizes.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.A',4,'As the AWG number gets smaller, the wire gets:','["Shorter", "Thicker", "Cheaper", "Thinner"]'::jsonb,1,'Lower number, larger wire.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.B',3,'Insulation on a conductor is selected based on the conditions such as:','["Weather forecast", "Brand names", "Temperature and moisture", "Color of walls"]'::jsonb,2,'Insulation matches environment.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.B',4,'A wire rated for wet locations is marked with the letter:','["D", "Q", "X", "W"]'::jsonb,3,'W means wet.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.C',3,'The voltage rating of a conductor is the:','["Maximum voltage it is designed to carry", "Color", "Minimum voltage", "Length"]'::jsonb,0,'Ratings limit voltage.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.C',4,'A cable rated for 600 volts should not be used on a:','["Lower voltage circuit", "Circuit with higher voltage", "120-volt circuit", "12-volt circuit"]'::jsonb,1,'Do not exceed rating.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.D',3,'Markings on cable jackets may show wire size, type, and:','["Color of the building", "Owner", "Voltage rating", "Age"]'::jsonb,2,'Markings carry data.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.D',4,'A cable marked 12/2 with ground has how many insulated conductors plus ground?','["12", "1", "3", "2"]'::jsonb,3,'12/2 means two 12 AWG conductors.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.E',3,'The ampacity of a conductor is found in the NEC:','["Tables", "Cover", "Index only", "Preface"]'::jsonb,0,'Ampacity tables.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.E',4,'Ampacity is the maximum current a conductor can carry:','["At any temperature", "Without exceeding its temperature rating", "Forever", "Without a load"]'::jsonb,1,'Ampacity limits heating.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.F',3,'Stranded wire is used because it is more:','["Fragile", "Brittle", "Flexible", "Expensive only"]'::jsonb,2,'Strands bend easily.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.F',4,'Stranded wire is better than solid wire where there is:','["No power", "No insulation", "No movement", "Vibration or frequent movement"]'::jsonb,3,'Strands resist fatigue.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.G',3,'Compressed conductors are made smaller in diameter by:','["Compacting the strands", "Painting", "Cutting", "Heating"]'::jsonb,0,'Compression reduces size.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.G',4,'A benefit of a compressed conductor is that it:','["Is weaker", "Takes up less space in conduit", "Is heavier", "Cannot be pulled"]'::jsonb,1,'Smaller diameter.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.H',3,'Common materials for conductors are copper and:','["Wood", "Plastic", "Aluminum", "Rubber"]'::jsonb,2,'Copper and aluminum.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.H',4,'Compared to aluminum, copper is generally a:','["Cheaper metal", "Lighter metal", "Worse conductor", "Better conductor"]'::jsonb,3,'Copper conducts better.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.I',3,'Conductor insulation types include THHN and:','["XHHW", "PVCQ", "ABCD", "ZXY"]'::jsonb,0,'Letter codes denote type.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.I',4,'The letter ''H'' in THHN indicates:','["Hard", "Heat resistance", "Humid", "Hot water"]'::jsonb,1,'H stands for heat.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.J',3,'In many systems, a green wire is used as:','["A hot conductor", "A switch leg only", "A grounding conductor", "A neutral"]'::jsonb,2,'Green is ground.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.J',4,'A white conductor is generally used as the:','["Hot wire", "Control wire", "Ground only", "Grounded (neutral) conductor"]'::jsonb,3,'White is neutral.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.K',3,'Instrumentation control wiring typically carries:','["Low-voltage signals", "Gas", "Water", "High-voltage power"]'::jsonb,0,'Control wiring is low voltage.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.K',4,'Shielded cable is used for instrumentation to reduce:','["Weight", "Electrical interference", "Color", "Cost"]'::jsonb,1,'Shielding blocks noise.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.L',3,'Equipment used for pulling wire includes a fish tape and:','["Spoon", "Whisk", "Pulling rope", "Knife"]'::jsonb,2,'Tools for pulling.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.L',4,'A cable lubricant is used to:','["Increase friction", "Dye wire", "Heat wire", "Reduce friction during pulling"]'::jsonb,3,'Lubricant eases pulls.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.M',3,'Before pulling wire, the conduit should be:','["Clean and free of obstructions", "Filled", "Dirty", "Closed"]'::jsonb,0,'Clear the path.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.M',4,'When pulling wire, the pulling force must not exceed the:','["Wire color", "Maximum tension rating of the cable", "Speed limit", "Weather"]'::jsonb,1,'Avoid damage.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.N',3,'Installing conductors in conduit follows the NEC rules on:','["Colors only", "Weather", "Fill and conductor count", "Prices"]'::jsonb,2,'Fill limits.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.N',4,'Leaving extra wire length at a box is needed to:','["Waste wire", "Add weight", "Look neat only", "Allow connections to devices"]'::jsonb,3,'Free conductor length.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.O',3,'To pull conductors in a conduit system, a technician often attaches wires to a:','["Fish tape", "Rope bridge", "Spoon", "Hose"]'::jsonb,0,'Fish tapes pull wire.','original'),
('127.109.d.8.O',4,'Two people pulling wire work best when one:','["Watches only", "Feeds the wire while the other pulls", "Pulls both", "Cuts wire"]'::jsonb,1,'Teamwork.','original'),
('127.109.d.9.A',3,'A design drawing is made up of views, notes, and a:','["Price list", "Cover letter", "Title block", "Menu"]'::jsonb,2,'Standard parts.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
